## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of concurrency in OS caches, race handling, and data integrity under contention.

## Key Concepts
- CAS-based locking and per-block loading state
- Wait queues and wakeups for concurrent readers
- Read-ahead decisions and eviction interactions

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch in C-like pseudo
typedef struct BlockEntry { inode_t *inode; int block_no; void *data; atomic_int loading; atomic_int ready; wait_queue_t wq; } BlockEntry;
BlockEntry *cache_lookup(inode_t*, int); void disk_read_async(inode_t*, int, void*);

void* get_block(inode_t *inode, int block_no) {
  BlockEntry *e = cache_lookup(inode, block_no);
  if (e && atomic_load(&e->ready)) return e->data;
  if (e == NULL) {
    e = allocate_entry(inode, block_no);
    if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&e->loading, 0, 1)) {
      disk_read_async(inode, block_no, e->data);
      atomic_store(&e->ready, 1);
      wake_all(&e->wq);
    } else {
      wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
    }
  } else {
    wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
  }
  return e->data;
}
```

